# Restore fork-only translations after upstream merges

## Why
This fork adds strings that upstream does not have — notably the custom date
filter operands (yesterday / tomorrow / this-last-next week / **next business
day**). Lingui catalogs (`packages/twenty-front/src/locales/*.po` and the
compiled `generated/*.ts`) come from upstream, so **every `git merge upstream/main`
overwrites them and drops these translations**. The UI then shows raw
message-id hashes (e.g. `J2StrG`, `o7gVQY`) in RU or English in UK/other locales.

The single source of truth for these fork-only translations is:
`packages/twenty-front/scripts/sync-custom-translations.cjs`

## What to do after EVERY merge of upstream/main
Run the combined target (extract → re-apply fork translations → compile):

```bash
npx nx run twenty-front:lingui:sync-custom
```

If the Nx daemon hangs in this environment, run the steps directly:

```bash
cd packages/twenty-front
npx lingui extract --overwrite --clean
node scripts/sync-custom-translations.cjs
npx lingui compile --typescript
```

Then commit the changed `src/locales/*.po` and `src/locales/generated/*.ts`.

## Verifying
Confirm the operands resolve (no hashes) in the compiled catalog, e.g. that
`generated/ru-RU.ts` contains `J2StrG` → `Вчера` and `o7gVQY` → `Следующий
рабочий день`, and that there are no `\u0000` (NUL) bytes in any `*.po`.

## Adding new fork-only strings
When a fork feature introduces a new translatable string that upstream lacks,
add its `msgid` (exactly as it appears in `en.po`) plus the per-locale
translation to `scripts/sync-custom-translations.cjs`, then re-run the target.
